  "intro": "The primary Charles Schwab alternatives for brokerage trading are Fidelity, Interactive Brokers, Webull, Firstrade and tastytrade. Each of these five brokerages lists $0 online commissions on United States (US) stocks and exchange-traded funds (ETFs), but they charge different rates for equity options, index options, broker-assisted trades and cryptocurrency transactions. At The Modern Wallet, we build financial tools and compare published fee schedules so you can evaluate the annual dollar cost of your trading activity.\n\n[Charles Schwab](https://www.schwab.com/pricing) charges $0 online equity trade commissions, $0 online base commissions plus $0.65 per options contract, and sets no account or trade minimums. Schwab also charges a $25 service fee for broker-assisted phone trades and collects 0.75% of trade value (minimum $0.01) on cryptocurrency buys and sells. Comparing these rates against alternatives helps isolate where per-contract charges, margin rates and transfer fees change your total bill.\n\nFor broader context across brokerage categories, see our guide on [what do brokers charge to trade](/guides/what-do-brokers-charge-to-trade/), our [best investment apps for beginners roundup](/roundup/best-investment-apps-for-beginners/), our [best brokers for treasury bonds roundup](/roundup/best-brokers-for-treasury-bonds/), and our [comparison hub](/compare/). If you are evaluating specific competing firms directly, consult our [Fidelity alternatives](/alternatives/fidelity/) review and our [Robinhood alternatives](/alternatives/robinhood/) guide.",
