  "chooseSection": {
    "heading": "How to Choose a Charles Schwab Alternative",
    "body": "Start your comparison with the asset classes you trade most often. Charles Schwab and all five alternatives provide $0 online commissions for US exchange-listed stocks and ETFs, though Interactive Brokers (IBKR) restricts $0 trades to its IBKR Lite plan for US residents. For options, contract charges create immediate cost differences. Charles Schwab charges $0.65 per contract, Webull and Firstrade charge $0 per contract on standard equity options, and tastytrade charges $1 per contract to open with a $10 cap per leg and $0 to close.\n\nNext, evaluate broker-assisted trading fees if you ever place phone orders through a representative. Charles Schwab adds a $25 service charge per broker-assisted trade. Firstrade charges $19.95 for broker-assisted stock and ETF trades, or $19.95 plus $0.50 per contract for options trades. On a 10-contract options trade placed by phone, Charles Schwab charges $0 base plus 10 times $0.65 ($6.50) plus $25, totaling $31.50. Firstrade charges $19.95 plus 10 times $0.50 ($5.00), totaling $24.95, which saves $6.55 compared to Schwab.\n\nThird, review margin interest rates if you borrow against your securities. Fidelity publishes a base margin rate of 10.825% effective since September 18, 2026, which steps down based on the debit balance tier. For example, a debit balance between $250,000 and $499,999 receives an effective margin rate of 10.325%. On a $300,000 margin loan balance maintained for one year, that 10.325% rate equals $30,975 in interest expense.\n\nFourth, check specialized transaction fees such as cryptocurrency spreads and account transfer fees. Charles Schwab charges 0.75% of trade value per buy or sell transaction on cryptocurrency with a $0.01 minimum. tastytrade charges 1% per purchase to open and close cryptocurrency positions, but lowers that fee to 0.75% for Bitcoin (BTC) and Ethereum (ETH). Webull lists an outgoing stock transfer fee of $75, which affects investors moving accounts out.\n\nCheck these four criteria when comparing firms:\n\n- How many equity options contracts do you enter and exit each month?\n- Do you require phone support to execute broker-assisted trades?\n- What size margin balance do you carry, and what published rate applies to that tier?\n- Do you trade cryptocurrency, broad-based index options or mutual funds?"
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "heading": "Fidelity Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Fidelity lists $0 online commissions on US stocks, ETFs and options trades, with options contract fees applying on top. Fidelity's published schedule notes that options contract fees apply, but does not list a specific dollar amount per contract on the checked pages. For margin borrowing, Fidelity publishes a base rate of 10.825% effective since September 18, 2026. This margin rate steps down for larger loan balances, reaching 10.325% for debit balances between $250,000 and $499,999.\n\nFidelity fits investors who prioritize $0 online stock trading and need tiered margin rates on six-figure borrowing balances.\n\nVersus Charles Schwab, Fidelity falls short on fee transparency for options contracts. Charles Schwab explicitly lists $0.65 per contract on its pricing overview, whereas Fidelity notes that contract fees apply without printing the per-contract dollar figure on its checked fee pages. Both brokerages offer $0 online equity commissions.\n\nWorked cost example: On a $300,000 margin loan balance, Fidelity's published 10.325% rate for the $250,000 to $499,999 tier results in an annual borrowing cost of $30,975 ($300,000 multiplied by 0.10325). For investors looking at equity trades alone, executing 50 online US stock transactions per year costs $0 at Fidelity and $0 at Charles Schwab."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Interactive Brokers Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Interactive Brokers provides two distinct pricing schedules: IBKR Lite and IBKR Pro. IBKR Lite offers $0 commissions on US exchange-listed stocks and ETFs for US residents. IBKR Pro uses volume-based pricing for stocks and ETFs, offering a choice between Fixed pricing at $0.005 per share and Tiered pricing ranging from $0.0005 to $0.0035 per share. For options on IBKR Pro, Fixed pricing costs $0.65 per contract, while Tiered pricing ranges from $0.15 to $0.65 per contract based on monthly volume. Interactive Brokers states that it requires no account minimums and charges no hidden fees.\n\nInteractive Brokers fits high-volume options traders who qualify for lower per-contract pricing through IBKR Pro Tiered schedules, as well as retail US investors seeking $0 commissions via IBKR Lite.\n\nVersus Charles Schwab, Interactive Brokers introduces per-share commissions if you trade on IBKR Pro rather than IBKR Lite. Charles Schwab charges $0 for all online equity trades without requiring users to pick between separate account pricing models.\n\nWorked cost example: Trading 200 options contracts in a single month on IBKR Pro Fixed costs 200 multiplied by $0.65, which equals $130. That matches Charles Schwab, where 200 contracts at $0.65 also cost $130. However, if that trading volume qualifies for the lower $0.15 rate on IBKR Pro Tiered, the options fee drops to $30 (200 multiplied by $0.15), saving $100 compared to Charles Schwab."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Webull Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Webull charges $0 broker commissions for US-listed stocks, ETFs and standard equity options, and lists no deposit minimums. Webull charges a $0.50 per contract fee on certain index options. Regulatory pass-through fees apply to trades, including a Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) fee of $0.0000206 times trade volume. Webull charges $75 per outgoing stock transfer if you move your portfolio to another institution.\n\nWebull fits self-directed options traders who trade standard equity options and want to avoid per-contract charges entirely.\n\nVersus Charles Schwab, Webull charges an outgoing stock transfer fee of $75. Webull also charges $0.50 per contract on certain index options, while Charles Schwab charges $0.65 on options contracts across the board.\n\nWorked cost example: Placing 100 equity options trades over a year costs $0 in broker contract fees on Webull. At Charles Schwab, those 100 contracts cost $65 (100 multiplied by $0.65). If you eventually transfer that account to an outside broker, Webull's $75 outgoing transfer fee offsets that options savings ($75 transfer fee minus $65 options fee equals a net cost of $10)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Firstrade Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Firstrade provides $0 commission trading for stocks, ETFs and options, and charges a $0 options contract fee. Firstrade also offers free mutual fund trading across load, no-load and no-transaction-fee (NTF) funds. For clients who execute trades over the phone, Firstrade charges $19.95 for broker-assisted stock and ETF orders, and $19.95 plus $0.50 per contract for broker-assisted options orders.\n\nFirstrade fits active options and mutual fund investors who want zero contract charges alongside lower telephone order fees.\n\nVersus Charles Schwab, Firstrade eliminates the $0.65 contract fee on self-directed options and reduces the base charge on broker-assisted transactions. Charles Schwab charges $25 for broker-assisted trades, while Firstrade charges $19.95 for stock and ETF phone orders.\n\nWorked cost example: An investor who trades 300 options contracts online and places two broker-assisted stock trades in a year pays $39.90 at Firstrade ($0 for options contracts plus two times $19.95). At Charles Schwab, those same transactions cost $245 (300 contracts at $0.65 equals $195, plus two phone trades at $25 each equals $50), making Firstrade $205.10 less expensive for that trading pattern."
    },
    {
      "heading": "tastytrade Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "tastytrade provides $0 commissions to open and close stock and ETF positions, and charges $0 for fractional shares. For equity options, tastytrade charges $1 per contract to open, capped at $10 per leg, and $0 per contract to close. Broad-based index options cost $1 per contract to open with no leg cap and $0 to close. For cryptocurrency, tastytrade charges 1% per purchase to open and close, reduced to 0.75% for Bitcoin (BTC) and Ethereum (ETH). The tastytrade pricing page explicitly states that there are no subscription fees.\n\ntastytrade fits options traders who trade large single-leg orders, such as blocks of 15 contracts or more, where the $10 cap limits opening costs.\n\nVersus Charles Schwab, tastytrade charges more on small options orders because opening a contract costs $1 versus $0.65 at Charles Schwab. However, tastytrade charges $0 to close options contracts and caps opening fees at $10 per leg. For cryptocurrency, tastytrade charges 1% on general crypto purchases (0.75% for BTC and ETH), while Charles Schwab charges 0.75% on all supported crypto buys and sells.\n\nWorked cost example: Opening and closing a single leg of 100 equity options contracts costs $10 at tastytrade ($1 per contract capped at $10 to open, and $0 to close). At Charles Schwab, opening and closing those same 100 contracts costs $130 (100 opening contracts at $0.65 equals $65, and 100 closing contracts at $0.65 equals $65). On that single order, tastytrade saves $120. Conversely, on a single-contract order, tastytrade costs $1 to open ($1 total), while Charles Schwab costs $0.65 to open ($1.30 round trip)."
