  "stillBetterFit": {
    "heading": "When Charles Schwab Is Still the Better Fit",
    "body": "Charles Schwab fits investors who want standard $0 online US equity trades, no account minimums, and consistent $0.65 options pricing across all contract volumes. Charles Schwab charges no monthly platform fees and no base commission on online equity trades. For investors trading small options orders of one to five contracts at a time, Schwab's $0.65 per-contract cost is lower than tastytrade's $1.00 opening fee whenever positions are held to expiration or assigned.\n\nCharles Schwab also provides cryptocurrency trading with a flat 0.75% fee per buy or sell transaction (minimum $0.01). On a $1,000 general cryptocurrency purchase, Charles Schwab charges $7.50 ($1,000 multiplied by 0.0075). tastytrade charges 1% on general cryptocurrency transactions ($10.00 on a $1,000 order), meaning Charles Schwab saves $2.50 on non-BTC and non-ETH crypto assets.\n\nWho This Is Not For:\n\n- Active options traders who place multi-contract block trades: tastytrade caps options opening commissions at $10 per leg with $0 to close, and Webull and Firstrade charge $0 per contract on standard equity options.\n- Investors seeking volume-based options pricing: Interactive Brokers Pro offers tiered options pricing from $0.15 to $0.65 per contract, which beats Schwab's flat $0.65 rate at high volumes.\n- Frequent phone traders: Charles Schwab adds a $25 service fee per broker-assisted trade, whereas Firstrade charges $19.95 for stocks and ETFs.\n- Crypto traders seeking zero-fee execution: Charles Schwab charges 0.75% per buy or sell, which adds $15 in total fees on a $1,000 purchase and subsequent $1,000 sale.\n\nWhat Would Change Our Answer:\n\n- Charles Schwab eliminating or discounting its $0.65 options contract fee on larger order sizes.\n- Webull introducing per-contract fees on standard equity options or increasing its $75 outgoing transfer fee.\n- tastytrade removing its $10 per leg commission cap on options trades.\n- Firstrade adding per-contract fees to self-directed online equity options orders.\n- Fidelity publishing lower flat per-contract options fees than Charles Schwab on its public pricing overview."
